# ---- the main-red re-test hold has an upper bound (BH-1475, optional) ----
# BH-1281 taught the streak arm of bin/main-red-detector.sh to HOLD rather than
# raise while the same workflow is being re-tested: two completed reds plus a
# newer run of that workflow still in flight means a fix is probably on its way,
# and re-raising would page a second time and give flags/main-red-<owner>-<repo>
# a new mtime — the very lower bound mrt_main_red_fixed reads for "has a fix
# merged since the freeze went up". That was right, and it had no upper bound.
# While a streak is held NO flag exists, so every merge lane reads "not frozen"
# and keeps merging onto a main whose last COMPLETED evidence is two reds. A
# re-test can sit in flight for an hour.
#
# So: how many DISTINCT main shas may land on top of the held sha before the
# hold ends early. At or past this many, the detector freezes on the HELD sha's
# failure (reason=held-overrun), writes head= and depth= the way the burst arm
# does, and logs which merges landed while the hold was up. Depth is counted
# from the runs page already in hand — a merge to main produces a run — so the
# arm costs no extra API call, and a sha with no run yet is not counted, which
# errs toward holding.
#
# 2 is the default on purpose. Depth 1 is exactly BH-1281's own shape (the fix
# merged, its own run in flight), and at 1 this arm would re-open that fight.
# At 2 a SECOND PR has landed on a main whose only completed evidence is red,
# and freezing is the right answer even though it costs that fix its
# batch-resolve lift — the freeze still clears on the fix's own green run.
# 0 disarms the arm, which is byte-for-byte the pre-BH-1475 decision: a held
# streak is then never bounded. A non-numeric or negative value falls back to
# 2 rather than to off, because a typo must not silently disarm a freeze.
#MAIN_RED_HOLD_OVERRUN=2
